  "metadata": {
    "title": "Tesla Stock Traded Its Affordable Car Dream For An AI Moonshot",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "As the company's core auto business shrinks, management has gone quiet on its promise of cheaper EVs, betting everything on a future that's years away and will cost billions.\nWhen you own Tesla (TSLA), what business do you actually own? The answer has changed, and the silence is how you know. For years, the story was about scale: building millions of electric cars for everyone. But as the company's massive automotive division has stalled, that story has quietly faded, replaced by a far grander, riskier vision for AI and robotics. The loudest signal on Tesla's last earnings call wasn't what was said, but what wasn't.\nThe Ghost Of The Everyman's EV\nNot long ago, the mission was clear and repeated constantly: drive down costs to bring electric vehicles to the masses. Management was explicit, telling investors they were \"on track to deliver a more affordable model\" and focused on getting the \"overall cost per car down below $35,000.\" This was the engine of the investment case, a world-changing manufacturing story about dominating the future of transportation through volume and accessibility.\nThat drumbeat has stopped. You hear far less now about that next affordable model or the cost-per-vehicle metric that once headlined their progress. The de-emphasis coincides with a hard reality in the numbers: the core Automotive segment, which still makes up 87% of Tesla's revenue, saw sales decline 6% over the past year. The story of unstoppable growth in the car business went quiet right as the growth itself disappeared.\nThe $25 Billion AI Wager\nIn its place is a breathtakingly ambitious new narrative. The company now leads with its future in artificial intelligence, from the Optimus robot, which the CEO believes will be the \"biggest product ever,\" to its own custom AI chips. The car itself has been reframed. In management's new telling, they now \"emphasize FSD as a product and vehicle as only the delivery mechanism.\" The center of gravity has moved from the factory floor to the data center.\nThis pivot is grounded in action, backed by a colossal spending plan. The company is preparing for \"over $25 billion of CapEx\" in 2026 to fund this new era. The contrast is the entire story: the focus has shifted from scaling a proven, $82.1 billion auto business to funding a speculative AI future with a $25 billion checkbook, just as the old business has begun to shrink.\nThe Quiet That Deserves A Closer Look\nThis shift is concerning. A story pivot is one thing; a pivot away from your primary revenue engine just as it stalls is another. While the long-term vision for AI and robotics is compelling, it's a wager that will burn cash for years. Meanwhile, the company's overall net margin has collapsed from a peak of 15.5% to just 3.9%, signaling deep stress in the existing business that's supposed to fund the moonshot.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "As the company's core auto business shrinks, management has gone quiet on its promise of cheaper EVs, betting everything on a future that's years away and will cost billions.",
      "When you own Tesla (TSLA), what business do you actually own? The answer has changed, and the silence is how you know. For years, the story was about scale: building millions of electric cars for everyone. But as the company's massive automotive division has stalled, that story has quietly faded, replaced by a far grander, riskier vision for AI and robotics. The loudest signal on Tesla's last earnings call wasn't what was said, but what wasn't.",
      "The Ghost Of The Everyman's EV",
      "Not long ago, the mission was clear and repeated constantly: drive down costs to bring electric vehicles to the masses. Management was explicit, telling investors they were \"on track to deliver a more affordable model\" and focused on getting the \"overall cost per car down below $35,000.\" This was the engine of the investment case, a world-changing manufacturing story about dominating the future of transportation through volume and accessibility.",
      "That drumbeat has stopped. You hear far less now about that next affordable model or the cost-per-vehicle metric that once headlined their progress. The de-emphasis coincides with a hard reality in the numbers: the core Automotive segment, which still makes up 87% of Tesla's revenue, saw sales decline 6% over the past year. The story of unstoppable growth in the car business went quiet right as the growth itself disappeared.",
      "The $25 Billion AI Wager",
